1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:04,800
Most cancer drugs fail before they reach a patient.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,752
For decades, cancer cells have been grown flat, in a

3
00:00:08,552 --> 00:00:09,590
plastic dish.

4
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:14,015
So labs now grow something closer to the real thing:

5
00:00:13,815 --> 00:00:17,335
tiny 3D copies of a patient's tumour, called

6
00:00:17,135 --> 00:00:18,090
organoids.

7
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:22,327
This year, two leading cancer centres used organoids

8
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:25,829
to switch off every gene, one at a time, and find the

9
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:28,010
ones a cancer can't live without.

10
00:00:28,410 --> 00:00:31,875
Hundreds of genes mattered more in organoids than in

11
00:00:31,675 --> 00:00:35,014
flat cells, and those are now being put forward as

12
00:00:34,814 --> 00:00:35,830
drug targets.

13
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:37,720
Here's the catch.

14
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:40,750
Organoids change two things at once.

15
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:44,850
One is the shape. 3D instead of flat.

16
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:47,560
The other is the food.

17
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:51,520
Cells in a dish live in a liquid called the medium.

18
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:55,657
Organoids get a clean, lab-made medium with almost no

19
00:00:55,457 --> 00:00:56,480
cholesterol.

20
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:00,658
So when a gene matters more in an organoid, which

21
00:01:00,458 --> 00:01:01,900
change caused it?

22
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,760
The data to tell them apart already existed.

23
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:09,434
A big public database had flat cells grown on the

24
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:10,470
organoid medium.

25
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:13,440
Same diet, no 3D shape.

26
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,477
If a gene matters more there too, the shape can't

27
00:01:17,277 --> 00:01:19,230
explain it. The food can.

28
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:25,910
With Muse, Meta's AI model, running the research loop,

29
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:28,700
we checked all 17,525 genes.

30
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,180
456 clearly behave differently in organoids.

31
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:37,875
So how many of those 456 also change when the only

32
00:01:37,675 --> 00:01:40,250
thing you change is the food?

33
00:01:42,350 --> 00:01:44,150
387.

34
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:46,390
Eighty-five percent.

35
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:51,862
So the effect may not come from the tumour's 3D shape

36
00:01:51,662 --> 00:01:54,780
at all. It may come from what we feed it.

37
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:58,953
And in a patient, a tumour is fed by blood, which is

38
00:01:58,753 --> 00:02:00,250
full of cholesterol.

39
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,246
So a weakness that only shows up on a diet with almost

40
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:07,500
no cholesterol may not exist in the patient at all.

41
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,260
But there's a simple way to check.

42
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,550
Put the cholesterol back into the organoid's food.

43
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:17,439
If these genes stop mattering, it was the food. If

44
00:02:17,239 --> 00:02:20,530
they still matter, they're stronger candidates.

45
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:24,690
All of this is free at itsthemedium.com.

46
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:28,629
Researchers can paste their own list of genes and see

47
00:02:28,429 --> 00:02:31,380
in seconds which ones look like diet effects.

48
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:35,893
For the follow-up test, Meta's SAM 3.1 outlines and

49
00:02:35,693 --> 00:02:39,460
measures every organoid in a lab's own microscope

50
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:39,970
images.

51
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:44,260
And Muse answers questions by searching the full table

52
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:46,780
with its own tools, showing every step.

53
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,390
Organoids are still a big step forward.

54
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:53,910
We just need to check what we're feeding them.

55
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:55,960
It's the Medium.

56
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,380
itsthemedium.com

